WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
STEVE BALLMER:
This is Steve Ballmer. He was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:10.880
CEO of Microsoft between 2000 and 2014 which gave&nbsp;
him a net worth of $112 billion. Seeing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.040
you’d probably be inclined to think that&nbsp;
Steve was one of the most respected tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:22.240
executives of all time but it’s actually quite&nbsp;
the opposite. In fact, aside from actual frauds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:29.320
and incompetent leaders, Steve Ballmer is often&nbsp;
viewed as the worst big tech CEO of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:35.800
and many attribute his wealth to nothing&nbsp;
more than dumb luck. Why you ask? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:41.200
for starters, Microsoft stock fell 36% under&nbsp;
his leadership which translates to a market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:47.880
cap loss of roughly $100 billion itself. This&nbsp;
was largely due to Microsoft screwing up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:53.080
smartphones and Windows 8 under his leadership.&nbsp;
In fact, the stock actually started rallying when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:59.000
Steve Ballmer announced his exit. But, even more&nbsp;
than stock performance, much of the tech community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.040
simply viewed Ballmer as a meme. There were all&nbsp;
these these infamous clips of him laughing at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:41.280
iPhone and going absolutely crazy on stage.&nbsp;
But, if we look past these funny clips and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:47.600
his goofy personality, we’ll see that Steve was&nbsp;
much more than just a tech executive. For Steve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:54.440
Microsoft wasn’t just a company that he led, it&nbsp;
was a company that he bled. He was fiercely loyal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.440 --> 00:02:14.000
to everything Microsoft as he truly loved&nbsp;
the company. He thought that Microsoft was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.160
hands down the best company in the world and that&nbsp;
everyone should use everything Microsoft. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.120
he would eventually go on to buy the Clippers&nbsp;
and he would force all of the players to ditch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:30.520
their iPads and iPhones. You could say that he&nbsp;
was basically the Louis Litt of Microsoft. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.440
it wasn’t just loyalty that Steve had going for&nbsp;
him. Fundamentally speaking, he actually did a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.520
pretty good job. In fact, under his leadership,&nbsp;
Microsoft’s revenues and profits more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:46.280
tripled. Of course, this was never reflected in&nbsp;
the stock price, but that’s not something that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:52.080
has control over. So, was Steve Ballmer actually&nbsp;
just an enthusiastic dud who got luck beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:59.760
belief or was he just a misunderstood leader who&nbsp;
more than deserved his $112 billion compensation?

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:09.280
NEAR MISS:
Microsoft’s $100 billion bet on Ballmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.680
is no doubt controversial but another $100 billion&nbsp;
bet that’s not controversial is their investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:20.400
in cash equivalents and short term investments or&nbsp;
mostly bonds. If you’re not familiar with bonds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.800
it’s a way to lend money to the top corporations&nbsp;
and governments in the world. These institutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.000
will then turn around and use your money to&nbsp;
fund expansion efforts, launch new products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.480
and grow their profits. And in return for helping&nbsp;
them with their expansion, these institutions will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:40.600
pay you back with interest. Traditionally, bond&nbsp;
investing is seen as something that conservative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:45.720
old people do to protect their savings. But,&nbsp;
thanks to the high interest rate environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:51.280
bonds are paying higher yields than ever. In fact,&nbsp;
1 year Bills from the US government are currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:57.360
yielding 5.5% and 10 year notes from the US&nbsp;
government are currently yielding 4.7%. If you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.440
like to take advantage of these high interest&nbsp;
rates and lock in high yields for yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.960
please consider checking out our upcoming bond&nbsp;
investing app Silo in the description below. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.800
anyway, getting back to Steve Ballmer, the story&nbsp;
of Steve actually goes all the way back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:17.440
1970s to the dorm rooms of Harvard. Maybe, you’ve&nbsp;
wondered what it would be like to know someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.760
like Bill Gates or Steve Jobs before they were&nbsp;
famous in school or college. Well, luckily for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:27.760
Ballmer, that’s exactly what would happen as he&nbsp;
would end up as across the hall from Bill Gates at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.440
Harvard. Hearing this, you’re probably thinking:&nbsp;
oh well, Bill and Steve probably founded Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:39.880
together. That must be why Steve got so ruch&nbsp;
but that’s not how things played out. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:45.040
Steve was from a relatively conservative immigrant&nbsp;
family who very much valued the stability of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:50.520
college degree and a 9 to 5 job. So, dropping out&nbsp;
of Harvard to bet it all on a software company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:55.920
with Bill wasn’t exactly Steve’s cup of tea.&nbsp;
So, when Bill dropped out, Steve would simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.980
wish him good luck and farewell and stick with&nbsp;
Harvard. He would eventually graduate magna cum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:06.240
laude with a degree in applied mathematics and&nbsp;
economics in 1977. And he followed this up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.800
a traditional job as an assistant product manager&nbsp;
at Proctor &amp; Gamble. As far as the general public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:17.960
was concerned, Steve had done it. He had a degree&nbsp;
from Harvard and a stable job at a legacy giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:23.760
but Steve couldn’t help but feel that something&nbsp;
was missing. Maybe it was the monotony of a 9 to 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:29.800
or the lack of creativity, he couldn’t tell but he&nbsp;
was determined to find out. In fact, he would turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.720
around and try to break into hollywood by writing&nbsp;
screenplays in his free time. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.960
this never went anywhere and Steve would find&nbsp;
himself lost once again. He figured that maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:45.000
returning to school would help him, so he would&nbsp;
enroll in a graduate program at Stanford. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.200
it wasn’t until Steve got a call from Bill that&nbsp;
he would finally find his path. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:55.880
Microsoft was filled with a bunch of extremely&nbsp;
smart tech dudes but what they didn’t have was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:01.360
a business dude. And as the company grew larger&nbsp;
and started have more meetings and negotiations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.080
this became increasingly apparent. Microsoft&nbsp;
needed a business leader so Bill would call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:12.240
up his friend from Harvard: Steve Ballmer. This&nbsp;
time, Steve wasn’t hesitant. After all, he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:18.000
nothing to lose. He already had a degree and real&nbsp;
world experience, so if Microsoft didn’t work out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.880
he could always just get another job or return&nbsp;
to Stanford. In fact, when Steve told his mom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:28.320
about his plan to join Microsoft she said quote&nbsp;
“OK, OK, we hear you, but if it doesn’t work out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:41.942
you’ll go back to business school right?” Steve&nbsp;
replied “right” but he would never come back.

00:06:41.942 --> 00:06:43.200
PROVING HIS WORTH:
Steve’s initial salary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:49.680
was quite modest, $50,000 per year but that&nbsp;
wasn’t the entire offer. Bill also offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:56.080
Steve 5-10% equity in the company depending on&nbsp;
his performance. Steve started off as basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.760
the scapegoat. Anything that the tech guys&nbsp;
didn’t want to do, Steve would handle. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:06.400
included little things like cooking and washing&nbsp;
bottles to setting up accounting and HR. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:12.360
it wasn’t until later on in 1980 that Steve would&nbsp;
really prove his worth. You see, in July of 1980,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.680
IBM would approach Microsoft with a deal&nbsp;
to make software for their new computers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:25.320
There was just one problem, Microsoft didn’t&nbsp;
actually have what they were looking for:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:31.840
an operating system. And this is when Microsoft&nbsp;
would pull off one of the biggest bluffs in tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:37.640
history. Though Microsoft didn’t have their own&nbsp;
OS, they did know where to find one. A local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:43.160
computer company called Seattle Computer Products&nbsp;
had an OS called QDOS which was very similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:55.360
what IBM was looking for. So, Microsoft’s plan was&nbsp;
to basically get IBM to allow Microsoft to go out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:02.760
and buy QDOS and modify it for IBM’s needs. But,&nbsp;
there was one obvious flaw with this plan. IBM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:08.000
could easily just go to Seattle Computer Products&nbsp;
directly. So, it was up to Microsoft to convince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:14.560
IBM to let them be this sort of middle man and&nbsp;
that’s where Steve Ballmer comes in to play. Steve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:20.600
would basically become Microsoft’s sales guy with&nbsp;
IBM. And let me just say, Steve hit it out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:27.440
park as this deal would basically make Microsoft.&nbsp;
IBM didn’t even know what was happening. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:31.800
they thought that they had pulled a quick one&nbsp;
over on Steve and these college dropouts. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:37.600
Microsoft didn’t ask for as much money upfront or&nbsp;
a per copy royalty like IBM was expecting them to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:44.120
So, they thought they had a slam dunk deal. They&nbsp;
didn’t have to pay as much and Microsoft was gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:50.760
handle all of the ground work in customizing QDOS&nbsp;
for IBM. And in return, all Microsoft wanted was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:56.520
the ability to sell this OS to other companies,&nbsp;
that’s it. Without giving it a second thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:03.120
IBM would agree to pay Microsoft $45,000 for&nbsp;
what would end up being MSDOS. On the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:09.960
Microsoft didn’t make a good deal as it would cost&nbsp;
them $50 to $75,000 just to acquire QDOS. But Bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:15.280
Gates very much knew what they had just pulled&nbsp;
off. They had basically gotten IBM to largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:20.360
pay for a new operating system that Microsoft&nbsp;
would own and IBM would popularize despite not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:25.480
even creating the software in the first place. It&nbsp;
wasn’t till decades later that the general public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:29.680
really began to understand what was really&nbsp;
at stake for Microsfot with this deal. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:36.360
Bill knew immediately and as promised, in 1981, he&nbsp;
would hand over an 8% stake to the man who made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.240
possible: Steve Ballmer. Over the next few years,&nbsp;
Microsoft would follow through with their deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:46.840
with IBM and deliver to them a customized&nbsp;
version of QDOS called MSDOS. MSDOS would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:53.200
end up being a solid success and lead to a very&nbsp;
successful IPO, but the real magic didn’t happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.200 --> 00:10:00.880
till 1985. In 1985, Microsoft would take MSDOS,&nbsp;
add in a graphical user interface, and call it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:15.263
Windows. And this would take MSDOS from being a&nbsp;
successful product to being a legendary product.

00:10:15.263 --> 00:10:17.720
STEVE’S LEGACY:
The rest of the 1980s and 90s were pretty smooth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:23.440
sailing for Steve. As Microsoft grew, so did his&nbsp;
wealth and title. He went from being a manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:28.000
to being an executive leading various divisions&nbsp;
across Microsoft including operations, sales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:33.160
OS development, and support. And all of this&nbsp;
climaxed when Bill Gates decided to step aside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:39.360
at the turn of the century, making Steve Ballmer&nbsp;
the new CEO of Microsoft which brings us back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:47.080
the question of the day: did Steve Ballmer deserve&nbsp;
to earn $112 billion? Well, that’s hard to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:52.400
frankly because it’s hard to say that anyone truly&nbsp;
deserves that sum of money, but we can look at it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:57.240
through a different lens. Instead of looking at&nbsp;
from an output perspective, we can look at it from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:04.480
an input perspective. Did Steve Ballmer make an 8%&nbsp;
contribution to Microsoft back in 1980? Well, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:09.960
answer is absolutely. He spearheaded the deal that&nbsp;
not only put Microsoft on the map but literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:17.040
led to the creation of their hero product:&nbsp;
Windows. So, no, Steve did not just get lucky. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:22.080
only reason people even say that is because Steve&nbsp;
didn’t immediately take the plunge with Bill Gates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:28.320
and Paul Allen in 1975. So, history has basically&nbsp;
painted Steve as this guy who just happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:34.720
come in as Microsoft’s 30th employee and got an 8%&nbsp;
stake just because he was friends with Bill Gates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:40.440
And his goofy bubbly personality simply plays&nbsp;
into this persona but this really isn’t a fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:46.160
assessment. While Steve wasn’t there from day 1,&nbsp;
he very much played the role of being a cofounder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:51.800
or atleast the role of being their first business&nbsp;
executive. And the IBM deal was simply his biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:57.640
contribution. Let’s not forget that Steve also&nbsp;
spent 34 years as a Microsoft executive 14 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:04.240
which were as CEO. And during his stint as CEO,&nbsp;
Microsoft came out with some of their most iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:10.280
products including the Xbox, Windows XP, Windows&nbsp;
7, and even Azure which is often quoted as being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:17.640
what “saved Microsoft”. Not to mention, Steve&nbsp;
tripled Microsoft’s revenue and profit. Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:22.240
stock not going anywhere had more to do with the&nbsp;
dotcom crash and the 2008 crash than it did with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:28.120
anything the Steve did or didn’t do. And it was&nbsp;
eventually the shareholders that pushed him out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:33.680
but he’s still very much loyal to Microsoft&nbsp;
to this day. In fact, he’s by far Microsoft’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:40.360
largest individual stakeholder retaining over half&nbsp;
of his original stake at 4.5%. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:47.880
that’s 5 times more than Bill Gates who only owns&nbsp;
0.9% of Microsoft today. So, not only did Steve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:52.680
Ballmer play a crucial role in the early days&nbsp;
of Microsoft but there’s no question that Steve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:58.120
is by far Microsoft’s most loyal executive.&nbsp;
In fact, he probably wouldn’t have left if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:05.080
it wasn’t for shareholders pushing him out. So,&nbsp;
does Steve Ballmer deserve $112 billion? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:10.760
I can’t answer that question but what I can say&nbsp;
is that Steve Ballmer didn’t just get lucky. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:15.520
deserves his money just as much as any of his&nbsp;
centibillionaire peers and that’s why he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:22.120
paid $112 billion. If you’d like to take advantage&nbsp;
of high interest rates and have the government and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:27.800
top companies pay you 4-7%, check out our upcoming&nbsp;
bond investing app Silo in the description below.

